0014-datachannel-sid: report the DataChannel's REAL sid when the channel opens

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place.  This patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it is
the fourteenth patch of that series; it stacks on top of 0013-heal-loop).

Why
---

The DataChannel never opened on the device, and the reason was visible in
libpeer's own code -- not in the engine:

  * the engine's `on_dc_open()` callback is wired to libpeer's
    `ondatachannelopen`, which in turn is wired to `sctp_onopen()`;
  * `sctp->onopen` fires when the SCTP **ASSOCIATION** comes up --
    sctp.c: SCTP_COOKIE_ECHO (internal impl), SCTP_COOKIE_ACK (internal
    impl), SCTP_COMM_UP (usrsctp impl);
  * the **channel** does not exist at that moment.  The peer's DCEP OPEN is
    parsed later, in `sctp_parse_data_channel_open()`, and THAT is where
    the sid and the label are born (`sctp_record_open()` fills the stream
    table).

So the association-up callback cannot carry a sid: there is none yet.  The
engine worked around that by reporting the sentinel -1, and the facade
then keyed its fallback channel by -1 -- which is why "the DataChannel
never opened" (open=false, sid=-1, zero DC messages received) and why a
send with sid=-1 became `uint16_t` 65535, a stream the cloud does not
have.

What
---

  * sctp.h / sctp.c: a new callback
    `void (*ondatachannel)(uint16_t sid, const char* label, void* userdata)`
    plus `sctp_ondatachannel()` to register it.  It is invoked from
    `sctp_parse_data_channel_open()` -- i.e. exactly when the peer's DCEP
    OPEN has been recorded and the sid+label exist.  The association-up
    callback is left untouched (no timing change for existing users).

  * peer_connection.c / peer_connection.h: the same callback one level up,
    `peer_connection_ondatachannelopened()`, wired in
    `peer_connection_ondatachannel()` next to `sctp_onopen()`.

  * peer_connection.c: `peer_connection_get_datachannel_sid()` -- the sid of
    the channel the peer opened, WITHOUT depending on the channel's label
    (`peer_connection_lookup_sid()` needs the exact label string; the cloud
    uses "webrtc_data_channel" but that is an observation, not a contract).

  * peer_connection.c: `peer_connection_get_sctp_rtt_ms()` -- the smoothed
    RTT of the SCTP transport itself.  `peer_connection_get_rtt_ms()`
    prefers the ICE candidate pair and only falls back to SCTP, so the
    DataChannel path had no RTT of its own.

Criterion (the value really moves)
---

  * before: the engine's datachannelopen event carries sid = -1, always.
  * after:  the engine's datachannel event carries the real sid and the
    real label of the channel the cloud opened, and the datachannelopen
    event that follows carries the real sid.
  * on the device the report must show a sid != -1 and a label of
    "webrtc_data_channel" (the label the official session shows in
    pc/analysis/data/official-session-20261024/datachannel-frames.json).

